# A LIVE failed-to-transpile stub must NAME ITSELF before aborting.
#
# Codegen guards these stubs with GNU's `error` attribute, intending the C
# toolchain to be the deadness oracle. But that attribute is diagnosed from the
# BACKEND, after optimization: at -O2 LLVM folds the call to the noreturn stub
# away and it never fires. Measured on one unchanged .c — `clang -O0` reports
# the call, `clang -O2` reports nothing, and neither `-fno-inline` nor
# `noinline` brings it back. Every real build is -O2, so a live stub used to be
# a bare rc=134 with no output anywhere, which is how one shipped in a release
# (issues/fixed/ftt-stub-error-attribute-does-not-fire-at-O2.md).
#
# The fixture builds at ReleaseSafe (-O2) deliberately: at -O0 the attribute
# fails the C compile and this runtime path is never reached, which would make
# the case pass before AND after the fix. Verified: it does exactly that.
#
# Keep only the INVARIANT substring. The message also names the mangled fn id,
# but grep -oE stores whatever the pattern matches, so including the id would bake
# it into the golden and renumber it on any evaluator change. Measured: the first
# cut did exactly that.
#
# 2026-09-20: this fixture's swallow is a DERIVE-generated body, and a failure
# inside auto-generated code is a hard check error now
# (issues/fixed/derive-clone-over-a-non-clone-field-is-swallowed-into-a-runtime-abort.md)
# — so the build fails at `derive(P, Lbl)` with the method miss, before any
# stub exists. That is the stronger outcome the stub path was a fallback for,
# and the golden pins it. The self-naming stub stays in codegen as defence in
# depth; no user-reachable swallow class is known to reach it any more (an
# unbound name and a derive body are both hard), so the second alternative
# below is kept only so a regression back to the stub path is still visible.
stdout_keep_match=derive on "P" failed: No method "[a-z_]+" on String|whose body failed to transpile
timeout=900
